## 1. INTRODUCTION
Successful development of new technologies to address energy and environmental challenges currently faced by our society will likely require the discovery of new materials.' One class of promising materials are metal-organic frameworks (MOFs).² Based on inorganic (nodes) and organic (linkers) building blocks, MOFs are porous crystalline materials  whose adsorption properties could be exploited for low energy-cost chemical separations,3,4 storage of gaseous fuels in vehicular applications,5,6 trapping of toxic compounds from air,7 and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture in power plants. Remarkably high porosities9 and surface areaslo make MOFs inherently attractive for these applications. However, it is the outstanding tunability of MOFs—where millions of MOF variations could be obtained through different building block combinations6—that has truly driven the interest in MOFs, with $^{60,000+}$ MOFs synthesized to date.11
On the other hand, the overwhelmingly large number of potentially synthesizable MOFs makes it a challenge to rapidly pinpoint an optimal MOF for a given application. Seeking to alleviate this issue, computational high-throughput screening has emerged as a powerful approach to evaluate large numbers of candidate MOFs.46,12,13 To be successful, this approach requires both access to large numbers of in silico MOF structures and accurate predictions of relevant MOF properties. For gas storage and separation applications, adsorption loadings are a key MOF performance metric and can be predicted via grand canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations. Accurate GCMC predictions require an accurate description of adsorbate-adsorbent interactions, which are typically described by a parameterized force field split into dispersion/repulsion and electrostatic interactions. These interactions are often described with a Lennard-Jones and a Coulomb potential, respectively. For high-throughput screening, generic force fields such as DREIDINGl4 and UFFl5 have been proven accurate enough to identify outstanding materials for applications where adsorption is mainly driven by dispersion interactions. Thus, simulation efforts have led to the synthesis of new MOFs for methane storage,'6 hydrogen storage,6 and xenon/krypton separation.4
Applications where adsorption is strongly influenced by electrostatic interactions (e.g. $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ adsorption) present a greater challenge due to the sensitivity of predicted adsorption loadings to the assignment of electronic charges to MOF atoms. For a given MOF, an “accurate” assignment of electronic charges typically relies on the calculation of the electrostatic potential (ESP) in the MOF pores via ab initio calculations on the MOF crystallographic unit cell. Then, electronic charges are assigned to MOF atoms to reproduce the ESP using fitting procedures such as REPEAT,'7 DDEC18 (DDEC actually is an AIM partitioning method including ESP in the cost function for fitting) and others.'9 These methods, however, can be too computationally demanding to be applied for high-throughput screening.Efforts to obtain DDEC charges for a $5000+$ MOF database have been reported by Nazarian et al.,20 but likely due to computational cost, charges were only obtained for ca. $50\%$ of the structures.
To address these issues, strategies to rapidly assign MOF charges that reproduce electrostatic interactions accurately (enough) continue to be proposed. Most of these methods share the spirit of the charge equilibration (Qeq) method by Rappé and Goddard,2l where atomic properties such as the ionization potential along with atom connectivity information are used to assign electronic charges via“electronegativity equalization." Wilmer et al.22 proposed the EQeq method to reduce the number of fitting parameters and eliminate the iterative nature of Qeq, while introducing some ad hoc parameters. Wells et al.23 proposed FC-EQeq and I-EQeq to eliminate the need for assigning formal charges to some atoms in EQeq. Martin-Noble et al.24 proposed ${\mathrm{EQeq^{+}}}$ to include an empirical correction to EQeq to consider covalent bonding effects. Kadantsev et al.25 proposed MEPO-Qeq, where Qeq parameters were re-fitted to reproduce the DFT-derived electrostatic potential in ca. 500 MOFs. Most recently, Collins and $\mathrm{Woo}^{26}$ proposed SQEMEPO, where parameters for the split charge equilibration $(\mathrm{SQE})^{27}$ method—an extension of Qeq that includes covalent bonding effects—were re-fitted using an analogous strategy to MEPO-Qeq. However, a potential concern with these methods is the robustness and transferability of the many fitted parameters outside the MOF training set.
As an alternative to the electronegativity equalization methods, Xu and Zhong28 proposed the connectivity-based atom contribution (CBAC) method, where a database of charges from ab initio calculations is created for atoms in different bonding environments. The database can be used to assign electronic charges to each MOF atom based on its first coordination “shell." CBAC, however, does not consider the influence from more distant atoms, such as in the second or third coordination shell.
For studies on a single MOF, it has been common for authors to calculate ab initio ChELPG charges29 on MOF-representative clusters30-34 (Figure 1)—especially when  the MOF crystallographic unit cell is too large for periodic ab initio calculations—and then to manually map the calculated charges back to the corresponding MOF atoms. However, the manual character of this strategy is a significant obstacle for use in highthroughput screening, which may involve hundreds or thousands of MOFs.
Following the spirit of the MOF-representative cluster method, we propose an automated charge assignment method based on ab initio calculations on MOF molecular building blocks. The main assumption for the proposed method is that MOF molecular building blocks present similar charges in all MOFs they constitute. The advantage of this approach is its potential compatibility with current high-throughput, computational MOF construction approaches, which rely on libraries of molecular building blocks that are assembled into MOFs using automated algorithms.6,l235-37Therefore, intheproosed approach, a library of molecular building blocks is constructed with “built-in” electronic charges determined from ab initio calculations. When the MOFs are constructed in automated fashion from different building block combinations, the MOFs "inherit" the charges from the building blocks and thus are ready for adsorption simulations that require calculation of electrostatic interactions. In particular, we demonstrate this approach to be compatible with our topologically-based crystal constructor (ToBaCCo） code,% which constructs MOFs by mapping molecular building blocks onto topological blueprints. Note that the spirit of the proposed approach is akin to rapid charge assignment strategies used by molecular force fields such as CHARMM,38 AMBER,39 OPLS,40 and TRaPPE.41 In these force fields, molecules are often constructed from molecular building blocks (e.g. functional groups) in that charges from a common pattern are transferred from one molecule to another. For instance, in these force fields, the same oxygen charges may be used for the carbonyl group in various aldehydes, but differ from charges used for the carbonyl group of ketones, carboxylic acids, and amides.
The current work is organized as follows. In Section 2 we provide details of the computational procedures. In Section 3, first, we describe the strategy (e.g. how to delimit the building blocks) to obtain electronic charges calculated on MOF molecular building blocks using density functional theory (DFT). Second, we implement the molecular building blockbased (MBBB) method to assign charges to $400+$ yet-to-besynthesized MOFs created with ToBaCCo, for which we also calculate charges based on the EQeq, CBAC and REPEAT methods(aspossible). Third, we determine the similarity/dissimilarity of MBBB, EQeq, CBAC and MBBB charges to REPEAT charges, which we use as the “standard." Fourth, we demonstrate the practical feasibility for using MBBB charges for high throughput screening of MOFs for adsorption applications. We accomplish this by evaluating the similarity between $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ D $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $\Nu_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ Henry's constants, and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ loadings estimated from simulations using MBBB charges to the same quantities estimated from simulations using REPEAT charges. Furthermore, we evaluate how similar the ranking of MOFs (a critical step in hierarchical high-throughput screening) is based on simulations with either charge assignment method.
## 2. COMPUTATIONAL METHODS
## 2.1.Automated MOF construction
MOF structures were obtained in the computer using the topologically based crystal construction (ToBaCCo） code,6,42 which has been previously used to construct $^{13,000+}$ MOFs based on 41 topologies.% Here, we constructed 427 functionalized MOFs based on 16 topologies. Briefly, we provided ToBaCCo with 16 topological blueprints and a set of compatible building blocks, which ToBaCCo maps onto the blueprint and connects to each other. The constructed structures (MOFprototypes)then underwent a molecular mechanics-based optimization using the Forcite module of Materials Studio,43 using the Universal Force Field.l5 We targeted the construction of MOFs with porosity in the $0.50\mathrm{~-~}$ 0.85 void fraction range since, in our experience, higher sensitivity of adsorption properties to MOF charges is seen in this range. This is probably due to adsorption in MOFs with too low void fractions being limited by small pores, and in MOFs with too high void fraction being less sensitive to electrostatic interactions. Details on the used building blocks and topological blueprints to construct the MOFs are provided in Figure S1.
## 2.2. Calculation of electronic charges
EQeq. Electronic charges based on the EQeq method were assigned to the MOFs using the EQeq code.44 During the calculations, we used the ad hoc parameters implemented by Wilmer and coworkers,22 namely, a global dielectric constant, ε, equal to 1.67 and an artificial electron affinity, $I_{o},$ , of hydrogen equal to $-2\mathrm{eV}$ ，
CBAC. Electronic charges based on the CBAC method were calculated for 85 out of the 427 constructed methods. The assignment was done with the help of the Pybel45 toolkit and Smarts46 patterns constructed from a fullist of previously published CBAC charges.28 342 out of constructed MOFs did not receive CBAC charges due to the current unavailability of CBAC charges for some bonding patterns. The sum of all CBAC charges in a MOF is usually non-zero. To enforce charge neutrality, the same charge was subtracted (or added) to each MOF atom as needed to cancel out the excess positive (or negative) charge. Usually this correction amounts to less than $0.05e$ to each atom.
REPEAT. Due to computational cost constraints, charges based on the REPEAT method were calculated only for MOFs with unit cells featuring less than 300 atoms, corresponding to 145 out of 427 constructed MOFs. To obtain the MOF electrostatic potential, we used spin-polarized, plane-wave density functional theory (PW-DFT) calculations with VASP 5.3.1.47-50 The generalized gradient approximation (GGA） functional by Perdew, Burke and Ernzenhof $(\mathrm{PBE})^{51}$ was used to describe electron exchange and correlation. The projected augmented wave (PAW) method5² was used to describe core electrons.A $400~\mathrm{eV}$ cutoff was used for constructing the solution to the Kohn Sham equations with Blochl waves. Convergence for the electronic energy was set at $10^{-6}\mathrm{eV}$ No structural optimization was performed. Calculations were performed at the $\Gamma$ point. Gaussian smearing53 with a $0.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ broadening was used. Once the electrostatic potential was obtained, the electronic charges were calculated using the REPEAT code.
MBBB. Molecular building block-based (MBBB) charges were obtained from density functional theory (DFT） calculations with Gaussian ${\tt89}^{54}$ on“properly capped" (Figure 1b,c), isolated building blocks (see Figure S2 for more details on how different building blocks are capped). The capping of the building blocks aimed to emulate the typical bonding environment of the building blocks in MOFs while keeping the DFT calculations affordable. Full optimizations were performed on organic building blocks, whereas partial optimizations (only relaxing capping groups) were performed on inorganic building blocks. After geometry optimization, the electrostatic potential surrounding the building blocks was calculated. Then, the ChELPG method29 was used to assign electronic charges to reproduce the potential. All these calculations used the B3LYP functional5556 in conjunction with the $6{-}31{+}\mathrm{G}^{\ast}$ basis set57 for non-metal atoms and the LANL2DZ basis set and pseudopotential58 for the metal atoms. The
ChELPG charges were then mapped onto the corresponding building blocks in the ToBaCCo building block library. During automated MOF construction, the charges from the building blocks were incorporated into the MOF. To enforce charge neutrality, the same charge was subtracted (or added) to each MOF atom that was not a metal or part of a functional group as needed to cancel out the excess positive (or negative) charge. Usually this correction amounts to less than $0.02e$ to each atom.
## 2.3. Calculation of adsorption properties
All adsorption properties were calculated using the simulation code RASPA 2.0.59 Dispersion/repulsion and electrostatic interactions were described by Lennard Jones (LJ) and Coulomb potentials, respectively. LJ parameters and atom charges for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ were assigned according to the TraPPE force field,60 for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ according to the Darkrim-Levesque force field,61 and for $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ according to TIP4P forcefield.62 LJ parameters for MOF atoms were assigned according to the Universal Force Field,'5 and MOF atom charges were assigned according to the methods described in Section 2.2.A $12.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ cutoff was used for the LJ interactions. Ewald summation was used for electrostatic interactions. 100,000 cycles of random adsorbate insertions were used to probe adsorption energetics and obtain Henry's constants according to the Widom method.63 During GCMC simulations, 5,ooo cycles were used for equilibration, followed by 5,oo0 cycles to calculate ensemble averages. Each cycle corresponds to $N$ Monte Carlo moves where $N$ is the maximum between 20 and the number of adsorbate molecules in the simulation cell.
## 3.RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
3.1. Rationale for assigning charges based on calculations on MOF molecular building blocks
In this section, the justification for the proposed strategy to assign charges to MOF atoms is given. To start, note that a MOF can be abstractly deconstructed into “nodular” and “connecting” building blocks.Nodular building blocks correspond to the MOF inorganic nodes or the topological “nodes" of MOF linkers. Connecting building blocks connect nodular building blocks. Figure lc shows the nodular and connecting building blocks used to construct the MOF in Figure la. Here, we will show that 1） the connecting building block charges are relatively unaffected by the nodular building blocks they are connected to, 2) the nodular building block charges are relatively unaffected by the connecting building blocks they are connected to, and 3） the building block charges are relatively similar whether the building blocks are isolated or connected to other building blocks.
Before proposing the strategy outlined in this work, we tried to identify patterns in building block charges. To do so, we obtained DFT-calculated charges on 49 connecting building blocks and two inorganic nodes (the $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O}$ corner and copper paddlewheel). The main observation was that atoms with the same connectivity (same nearest neighbors) can present large variations in electronic charge (this was especially apparent for carbon and hydrogen atoms). Therefore, we completely discarded the possibility of assigning MOF charges based on atom connectivity as the CBAC method does. Accordingly, the next step was to determine whether DFT-calculated charges in isolated building blocks were representative of the charges of the building blocks when connected within the MOF structure.
## 3.1.1 Electronic charges for connecting building blocks
Unfunctionalized building blocks. To examine charge assignment for unfunctionalized, connecting building blocks, we selected 11 connecting building blocks (Figure 2） with representative features such as benzene rings, triple bonds and heterocycles. Then, DFT-calculated charges were obtained for the isolated building blocks as well as for MOF-representative clusters containing these building blocks. The goal was to see how the building block charges were affected by the bonding environment they encounter in typical MOF structures. The MOF-representative clusters were classified as either inorganicorganic-inorganic (IOl) clusters (Figure 3a,c) or inorganicorganic-organic clusters (IOO) (Figure 3b,d).
In the IOI clusters, the connecting building block is connected to one inorganic node at each end, representing the scenario encountered in MOFs based on ditopic linkers (e.g. IRMOF-1 or the MOF in Figure 1a). In the IOO clusters, the connecting building block is connected to an inorganic node at one end and to a methyl group at the other end. These clusters represent the scenario encountered in MOFs based on polytopic linkers (e.g NU-125), where the linker arms connect the inorganic node to the organic node (see Figure S3 for details). The methyl group is used to represent the organic character of the organic linker node. A comparison between the charges for the atoms in an isolated building block and the same atoms in the IOI and IOO clusters is presented in Figure 3 for the case of the phenyl building block. Parity plots for other building blocks are presented in Figure S4. The differences between the isolated building block charges and those in the clusters were quantified with the root mean square error (RMSE), which for the phenyl building block ranged from 0.038 to 0.104.Similar results were obtained for the other ten building blocks. The average RMSE was 0.0981. Unsurprisingly, the largest variation was for C1 (Figure 3), which changes from being an $\mathfrak{a}$ -carbon when connected to an inorganic node to being a “regular” carbon when connected to an organic node.
Functionalized building blocks. To examine charge assignment for functionalized, connecting building blocks, we focused on different functionalized versions of the parent phenyl ring building block. We chose this short connecting building block because possible charge distribution effects by the functional groups were expected to be more significant than in longer connecting building blocks. The studied functional groups were fluorine (-F), bromine (-B), cyanine (-CN), hydroxyl (-OH), thiol (-SH), amine $\left(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\right)$ ，nitro $\left(-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right)$ ， methyl $\left(-\mathrm{CH}_{3}\right)$ ,and fluoromethyl $\left(-\mathrm{CF}_{3}\right)$ . Depending on steric constraints, each of these functional groups was incorporated in up to two different configurations (Figure S6), one of which presents the phenyl ring fully functionalized. This resulted in 12 total functionalizations of the phenyl ring, for which we obtained DFT-calculated charges for the isolated building block and the corresponding IOI and IOO MOF-representative clusters based on copper paddlewheels and zinc corners.
A comparison between the charges in the isolated building block and the same atoms in the IOI and IOO clusters is presented in Figure 4 for fluorinated phenyl ring cases. It is particularly important to obtain charges for MOF functional groups that accurately reproduce the ESP around them, because the functional groups can have a significant impact on adsorption of polar molecules. For instance, these groups can be used to tailor electrostatic “hot spots"64,65 that enhance interaction energies of polar adsorbates within the MOF pores. Thus, the parity plots in Figure 4 show desirable results, because there is negligible variation between the charges of fluorine atoms in the isolated building blocks relative to the same atoms in the IOI and IOO clusters. The variation for other atoms is also small. For all atoms, the small variation is quantified by an RMSE in the $0.016-0.052$ range. As for the unfunctionalized building block, the largest variation was
observed for C1 (the $\mathfrak{a}$ -carbon). In summary, based on the estimated parity plots (Figure 3-4), we deemed DFT-calculated charges on isolated building blocks to be a reasonable representationofchargesoffunctionalizedand unfunctionalized, connecting building blocks when connected to other building blocks within a MOF structure.
## 3.1.2. Electronic charges for nodular building blocks
To examine charge assignment for nodular building blocks, we focused on the copper paddlewheel (Figure 5), $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O}$ (Figure S5), and triazine nodes (Figure 6). Analogous to our analysis for the connecting building blocks, we examined the DFTcalculated charges for the isolated nodular building block and the same atoms in MOF-representative clusters.
For the zinc- and copper-based nodes, the MOF-representative clusters correspond to IOI and IOO clusters with different connecting building blocks (Figure 5). The connecting building blocks correspond to the ones listed in Figure 2, but also include fluorinated building blocks to test the effect of groups that may induce significant charge redistribution. As quantified by the RMSE, which did not exceed 0.0930 and 0.0660 for the copper paddlewheel and the zinc oxide corner, respectively, the connecting building block had little effect on the nodular building block charges (Figure 5 and Figure S5). As expected, the largest variation comes from the C1 carbon (the carbonyl carbon) and to a lesser extent to the O1 oxygen (the carbonyl oxygen). The smallest variation was for the metal atom (copper or zinc), whose charge was essentially the same whether in the isolated building blocks or in the MOF-representative clusters.
For the triazine node, the MOF-representative clusters are shown in Figure 6, which includes a test extending the cluster to include inorganic nodes. RMSE values comparing the triazine charges in the MOF-representative cluster and in the isolated building block show that the node charges are not impacted much by the nature of the connecting building block or neighboring nodes. Therefore, on the basis of the small RMSE values, we deemed charges calculated in isolated nodular building blocks to be acceptable for use in nodular building blocks when connected to other building blocks within the MOF structure
Note that from a computational MOF construction perspective, the carboxylate moiety could be considered part of either the nodular or the connecting building block. We find that the charges of carboxyl carbon and oxygen at the inorganic/organic connection in MOFs are much closer to those in the isolated inorganic building block (with added carboxylate moieties) than in the isolated connecting building block (capped with carboxylic acid), which further supports the strategy of including the carboxylate moieties as part of the inorganic building blocks. Here we focused on MOFs based on oxygenmetal coordination, but we expect a similar strategy to be necessary for linkers based on nitrogen-metal coordination.
Based on the RMSE for parity plots in Figures 3-6, we conclude that DFT-calculated charges obtained in isolated connecting and nodular building blocks can be transferred to MOFs, provided that the isolated building blocks are“properly? capped.For satisfactory results, connecting, inorganic nodular, and organic nodular building blocks must be capped with carboxylic, methyl and phenyl groups, respectively, during DFT calculations. The charges assigned to MOFs atoms based on these calculations are referred to as molecular building blockbased (MBBB) charges.
## 3.2.Automated construction of MOFs and electronic charge assignment
The results discussed in Section 3.1 indicated that MBBB charges should be sufficiently accurate for use in adsorption simulations, especially in the context of computational highthroughput screening. To further explore this, we constructed MOFs of acs, bcs, bcu, fcu, flu, ftw, lvt, nbo, nia, pcu, pyr, reo, rhr, rht, ssb, and stp topologies and assigned charges to them based on the MBBB method, as well as the CBAC, EQeq, and REPEAT methods (as possible), with REPEAT used as the standard against which to compare the other methods. We targeted a topologically diverse set of constructed MOFs to compare the accuracy of the MBBB, CBAC, and EQeq methods for different spatial arrangements of the building blocks in the MOF—which may affect the electrostatic potential in the MOF pores, and thus the obtained REPEAT charges. To maintain the number of studied MOFs under 500 structures (427),we focused on MOFs based on functionalized versions of the connecting phenyl building block.
The phenyl building block was selected because it tends to result in structures with void fractions in the $0.50\textmd{-}0.85$ range, which, as noted in Section 2.1, is a range in which adsorption is rather sensitive to MOF charges. Twelve functionalized versions of the phenyl building block were obtained based on fluorine (-F), bromine (-B), cyanine (-CN), hydroxyl (-OH), thiol (-SH), amine $\left(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\right)$ ，nitro $\left(-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right)$ ，methyl $\left(-\mathrm{CH}_{3}\right)$ ，and fluoromethyl $\left(-\mathrm{CF}_{3}\right)$ . We also used five inorganic nodular building blocks based on common MOF inorganic nodes $\mathrm{(Cu_{2}(C O_{2})_{4}}$ 。 $Z_{\mathrm{{n_{4}O(C O_{2})_{6}}}},$ $\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{6}$ and $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H_{4})(C O_{2})_{n}([12-}n]\bullet H_{2}O\mathrm{-OH)}$ (where $n=12$ or 8) and six organic nodular building blocks. The structure of the zirconium-based building block was based on the structure proposed by Cramer and coworkers.66 For the nodes that had not been considered in previous sections, we obtained DFTcalculated charges for them based on the MBBB method (see Figure S1 for full list of building blocks used in MOF construction).
A library of building blocks with “built in" electronic charges was then prepared for use with ToBaCCo.To obtain the charges, connecting building blocks were capped with carboxylic groups, organic nodular building blocks were capped with phenyl groups, and inorganic nodular building blocks were capped with methyl groups. The required modified version of ToBaCCo (which can handle charge information in building blocks), generated MOFs, as well as used building blocks with built-in charge information are available in Github.38.67 During construction, the code retains the charge information of the building blocks, which is inherited by the computationally assembled MOF.Since the building blocks are not charge-neutral, the sum of all charges in a constructed MOF is usually not exactly zero. Thus, systematic corrections to MOF charges were applied to enforce charge neutrality (see Section 2). As detailed in section 2, CBAC, REPEAT and EQeq charges were calculated for 85, 145 and 427 MOFs, respectively. Due to the limitations of each method, only 33 MOFs had charges calculated with all four methods. Therefore, four-way comparisons were only possible for 33 MOFs and are discussed in Section 3.3. Data for a three-way comparison for 145 MOFs with MBBB, EQeq, and REPEAT charges is shown in Figure S11.
## 3.3. Comparison of electronic charges amongst different charge methods
Since electronic charges are not experimental observables, we take the REPEAT charges as our standard, against which we compare charges calculated from MBBB, CBAC, and EQeq. The four-way parity plots in Figure 7 compare the charges of 6007 atoms in 33 MOFs from these three methods versus those from REPEAT. Note that, considering all atoms, MBBB charges present an RMSE $37\%$ and $23\%$ lower than EQeq and CBAC charges. For carbon, which is the most abundant element in MOF structures, MBBB charges present an RMSE $28\%$ and $29\%$ lower than EQeq and CBAC charges. Improvements were also observed, for instance, for halogen atoms, where MBBB charges present an RMSE $84\%$ $(48\%)$ and $27\%$ $(22\%)$ lower than EQeq and CBAC charges for fluorine (bromine). As an example of charge assignment in metal atoms, MBBB charges present a RMSE $54\%$ and $33\%$ lower than EQeq and CBAC charges for zirconium atoms. Three-way comparisons (excluding CBAC: Figure S11） for 145 MOFs present similar trends as those in Figure 7. The results presented in Figure 7 support the molecular building block-based method as a computationally affordable strategy to assign DFT-quality charges to MOF structures for use in adsorption simulations. Since the charges are calculated on small molecular building blocks that are used to assemble MOFs, the usefulness of the method becomes increasingly apparent in high-throughput scenarios where large numbers of MOFs are generated and evaluated on the computer.
## 3.4 Comparison of predicted adsorption properties
Given that the motivation for developing affordable charge assignment methods is to correctly predict adsorption properties, we now proceed to compare predictions of these properties for MOFs with MBBB, CBAC, EQeq and REPEAT charges.
Henry's constants. The Henry's constant is the slope of an adsorption isotherm at low loading. Since calculating the Henry's constant is rather inexpensive, this calculation has been used as the first step in hierarchical high-throughput screening of porous materials for adsorption applications, to obtain a subset of structures on which GCMC simulations can be performed to predict adsorption at higher loadings. This approach is especially useful when the number of structures and/or the computational cost for the GCMC simulation per structure makes “brute force" high-throughput screening prohibitive. For instance, Bai and coworkers68 calculated preliminary branched $C_{\mathrm{n}}/$ linear $C_{\mathfrak{n}}$ adsorption selectivities ( $({\mathfrak{n}}=18-30)$ based on the ratio of Henry's constantstopre-screen thousandsofzeolitesfor hydroisomerization catalysis. Moghadam and coworkers69 used $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ Henry's constants to rapidly discriminate hydrophobic from hydrophilic MOFs among more than 130,000 structures.
Figure 8 presents parity plots comparing the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ? $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ D $\Nu_{2}$ D and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ Henry's constants predicted from simulations with MBBB, CBAC, EQeq charges versus those from simulations with REPEAT charges. For each parity plot, the Spearman rank correlation coefficient (SRCC) is presented. The Spearman rank correlation coefficient ranges from $^{-1}$ to 1 and indicates how similar/dissimilar the ranking of structures is based on a given property. The closer the SRCC is to 1, the more similar two rankings are. The SRCC is a highly relevant metric for computational high-throughput screening, where one of the goals is to rank prospective materials from most to least promising for further study. Notice that SRCC values are much closer to 1 $(0.957-0.989)$ for MBBB than for the other methods, especially for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ， $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ ，and $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ ，which are much more affected by electrostatic interactions than $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Additional error measures, the root mean square error (RMSE) and the mean absolute percent error (MAPE), are also presented in Table S2.
As Figure 8 shows, $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ 。 $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ ，and $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ Henry's constants calculated using MBBB charges are numerically closer to those from simulations using REPEAT charges than Henry's constant calculations using CBAC and EQeq charges are. This is quantitatively reflected on the corresponding RMSE and MAPE values (Table S2). Thus the more accurate MOF rankings from simulation using MBBB charges is a consequence of overall more quantitatively accurate predictions (i.e. predictions closer to those obtained with simulations using REPEAT charges). The most inaccurate predictions were for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ Henry's constants from simulations using EQeq charges, resulting in a significantly different ranking of MOFs than what is obtained with REPEAT charges $\mathrm{(SRCC=0.087}$ ，essentially showing no correlation). For $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ Henry's constants, there are no significant differences with the different charge assignment methods. $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ Henry's constants calculated from simulations using MBBB, CBAC, and EQeq charges all agree very well with the predictions using REPEAT charges. This observation mostly reflects that electrostatic interactions have little impact on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption in MOFs.
