Notice in Figure 10 (20 bar) that yellow points (low void fractions) aggregate at the low loading part of the parity plots while remaining close to the parity line. On the other hand, the blue points (high void fractions) spread at the high loading part of the parity plots and, at least for simulations with CBAC and EQeq charges, tend to deviate from the parity line. $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption at this high pressure is relevant for pre-combustion carbon capture. Despite the influence of void fraction, it is still clear that simulations using MBBB charges are more accurate than those using CBAC or EQeq charges. The RMSEs for simulations using the former are more than $80\%$ smaller than for simulations using the latter. Importantly, at all pressures, simulations using MBBB charges can be expected to produce similar adsorption-based MOF rankings to those that would be obtained with simulations using REPEAT,as the SRCC for simulations using MBBB charges is higher than 0.96 for all pressures.
