## CONCLUSIONS
We also selected three MOFs that presented the highest variability among simulation loadings across the different charge methods and tried to elucidate the origins of the difference in the simulations. Simulation snapshots (Figure S12) and probability density profiles (Figure S13-S15) indicate that the strongest adsorption sites for simulations using REPEAT or MBBB charges are similar, but these sites change when using CBAC or EQeq charges. Moreover, an energy analysis (Figure S16) suggests that adsorbate-adsorbent electrostatic interactions are often overestimated in simulations using CBAC or EQeq charges (especially the latter). This overestimation with CBAC/EQeqcreateelectrostatic “hotspots” at different locations than with MBBB/REPEAT
We have demonstrated a method to assign electronic charges to MOF atoms for use in adsorption simulations. The method is based on DFT calculations on the molecular building blocks used to computationally assemble MOFs. The presented analysisshows that, despite the significantly lower computational cost, the molecular building blocked-based (MBBB) charges are comparable to electronic charges from DFT calculations on full crystallographic MOF unit cells and produce similar adsorption property predictions. Due to its compatibility with automated MOF generation approaches, the MBBB approach is well suited for computational highthroughput MOF construction and screening. We anticipate that the approach will be useful in high-throughput screening of
MOFs for adsorption-based applications strongly influenced by electrostatic interactions, such as carbon capture, methane purification, and heat pumps.
